  "source_dataset": "MIMIC_IV_NOTE",
  "text": "Allergies:\nacetazolamide\n\nChief Complaint:\nLightheadedness and hypotension\n\nHistory of Present Illness:\n___ presents to OSH from PCP ___ w/ lightheadedness and \nhypotension the ___.  Her blood pressure was noted to be ___ \nat home.  She took her home antihypertensives that morning.  She \nreported intermittent abdominal pain with a lot of bloating.  Of \nnote, she was treated for H. pylori infection in ___ after \na positive h. pylori IgG test in ___.  She denied any nausea, \nvomiting, melena or BRBPR.  She has never had a colonoscopy.  \nShe denied taking any NSAIDs or anticoagulation.  She has had a \n20 pound weight loss in the past 2 months.\n\nPer report her baseline at a prior hospitalization was 27.4.  \n\nThe patient was initially fluid responsive in the ED.  At \n___ she received 2 u prBCS overnight with a stable BP \n120-140.  Hct prior to EGD was 30.9.  CT abd/pelv showed no \nevidence of retroperitoneal bleed.  Pt underwent EGD/colonoscopy \non ___.  During this procedure, the patient was noted to \nhave an active duodenal bleed.  Her BP dropped to the ___, \nbleeding was temporized with clips.  The patient was placed on a \nneo drip, intubated, given 4u pRBCs, 2.5L fluid.  The patient \nwas transferred to the ICU.  Shortly after transfer she no \nlonger required pressors, maintained good systolics in 120s.\n\nBrief Hospital Course:\nMs. ___ was admitted to the ACS service from OSH with HPI as \nstated above.  She underwent ___ embolization of the GDA on \n___ with ___ without incident.  She was kept intubated and \nreturned to the intensive care unit in stable condition.  \n\nOn post-op day 1, ___, her hematocrit levels were noted to \nbe stable and she was weaned from the vent.  She was extubated.  \nHer proton-pump inhibitor drip was weaned and she was placed on \nan IV PPI.  Her central venous line was also discontinued.  \n\nOn ___ she continued to progress.  She was transferred to \nthe floor and started on a simple diet and subsequently \nadvanced.  Her h. pylori history was at that time unknown except \nthat she had been treated for h. pylori infection previously in \nthe year; her H. pylori antibody blood test at ___ was noted \nto be positive.  It was unclear whether she had had completed \nher treatment and the patient was uncertain, and so she was \nbegun on ___ on a triple therapy regimen of amoxicillin, \nlevofloxacin, and pantoprazole.  She was ordered for a stool H. \npylori antigen test to help to determine whether the infection \nhad previously been cleared, but she did not produce stool to \nsend for analysis.  Her foley was discontinued.  She took good \noral intake on a regular diabetic diet and her hematocrit levels \nand vitals continued to remain stable.  Her sub-Q heparin was \nresumed on ___ as well.  She remained without evidence of bleed \nor of poor perfusion.  \n\nShe did produce a small amount of dark sputum on the morning of \n___ and this was sent for gram stain and culture; the \nspecimen was read as contaminated by oral flora and the patient \nwas unable to produce another sample.  A chest x-ray revealed an \narea concerning for left lower lobe pneumonia; as she was \nafebrile, saturating well on room air, and was breathing well \nand without reduced or otherwise concerning breath sounds, and \nalready on levofloxacin and amoxicillin for her presumed h. \npylori infection, it was decided that she would remain on her \ncurrent antibiotic regimen and follow up.  Her chest x-ray \nremained stable on ___, her day of discharge, and she \nremained afebrile without any additional episode of sputum \nproduction.  \n\nOf note, records from OSH noted CT read with findings of a lytic \nlesion within the left superior acetabulum which may represent a \nbone cyst or lytic metastasis.  The patient will follow up with \nher PCP, ___ and has an appointment scheduled for \nthe ___ following her discharge for this purpose.  Dr. \n___ was contacted and is aware of the CT findings.  \n\nOn the day of her discharge, Ms. ___ passed ~10ml of dark \nmaterial appearing to be old clotted blood.  This was examined \nby the physician and it was noted that she had not passed a \nbowel movement since before her embolization.  Her hematocrits \nremained stable and she remained asymptomatic.  It was decided \nthat the blood was most likely old hemolyzed and oxidized blood \nfrom before her embolization.  She expressed understanding of \nthe need to return to the hospital for any symptoms of \nhypotension, which were discussed with her and family members in \nthe room.  She is also discharged with docusate sodium \nprescription.  \n\nMs. ___ is discharged to home with previous ___ services in \nstable condition on ___ with appropriate prescriptions, \nappropriate directions, and instructions to follow up with her \nprimary care provider and to make an appointment to follow up \nwith ___ clinic.  She and her family expressed understanding of \nher care and plans.\n\nDischarge Diagnosis:\nDuodenal bleed",
